<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meroë</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meroe was an ancient city on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a> from around the sixth century BCE. It was a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-smelting">iron production</a> and trade, famous for its pyramids. Meroe declined in the fourth century CE, as the rising Aksumite kingdom extended its power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meroe.jpg" alt="Meroë" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wufei07 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NubianMeroePyramids30sep2005(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The floodplains supported barley, wheat and, later, sorghum, sustaining rural communities and towns such as Napata and later <b>Meroë</b>, while cattle herding remained central to wealth and status.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Makuria was one of three kingdoms that took shape in the Middle Nile valley after the decline of the Kushite Empire centred on <b>Meroë</b> in the fourth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Kerma traditions survived beneath Egyptian rule and fed into the later Kushite Empire of Napata and <b>Meroë</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kerma">Kerma</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Kushite Empire of <b>Meroë</b> collapsed in the 4th century, the Noubades (Nobatae), a people settled in Lower Nubia, formed a kingdom between the First and Third Cataracts.</blockquote><a href="/state/nobadia">Nobadia</a></li></ul></section>
